CIVETWEB SECURITY NOTES
=======================

This directory contains a stripped and locally patched copy of CivetWeb.
Security reports against the full upstream project are not automatically
applicable to snapraid-daemon.

This file tracks the CivetWeb security and HTTP framing problems reviewed
against the code actually present in snapraid-daemon. General robustness bugs
without a verified security impact are outside the scope of this file.

REVIEW BASELINE
---------------

Original CivetWeb upstream baseline: 4a4f0c95cd3bd7572cd23fd89ca6eef380d41b99

Commit:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/commit/4a4f0c95

Applicability is determined from the stripped source and from trim_civetweb.py
and Makefile.am, not from the CivetWeb version string.

The review covered all upstream CivetWeb issues and pull requests created from
v1.16, released on 2023-04-10, through 2026-09-21: 156 issues and 127 pull
requests. Runtime changes were checked against the stripped source to identify
security-relevant defects that are resolved locally or not applicable.

Correctness, portability, build, and robustness issues without a verified
security impact are intentionally not listed here.

Important disabled or removed features:

  NO_SSL=1
  NO_SSL_DL=1
  NO_CGI=1
  NO_FILES=1
  NO_FILESYSTEMS=1
  USE_LUA=0
  USE_WEBSOCKET=0
  USE_HTTP2=0
  USE_ZLIB=0
  MG_EXPERIMENTAL_INTERFACES=0
  Form handling removed


RESOLVED
========

1. Malformed or ambiguous HTTP request headers
-----------------------------------------------

Severity: SIGNIFICANT in snapraid-daemon

The vendored CivetWeb accepted several malformed or ambiguous request-header
forms that could change request framing or authority interpretation.

Content-Length was parsed with strtoll() without requiring the entire field
value to be valid. For example, a value such as 0x was accepted as zero.
Multiple Content-Length fields were also not rejected.

This was significant for snapraid-daemon because a non-empty JSON request
could be exposed to the REST layer with a length of zero. For the undelete
operation this could change an intended filtered recovery into the
empty-request behavior, which attempts recovery of all missing files.

CivetWeb also accepted a request containing both a non-identity
Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length, selecting chunked framing while ignoring
Content-Length. Accepting both creates ambiguous HTTP message framing and can
enable request desynchronization when another HTTP peer interprets the same
request differently.

Multiple Transfer-Encoding fields were likewise accepted while only the first
one was considered for request framing. A request containing
Transfer-Encoding: identity followed by Transfer-Encoding: chunked could
therefore make a chunked JSON body appear absent to the REST layer. For
undelete, this could discard the requested file and disk filters and schedule
recovery of all missing files.

Multiple Host fields were also accepted while only the first one was used.
Since Host determines request authority and domain routing, accepting multiple
values creates an ambiguous request. HTTP/1.1 requests without a Host field
were accepted as well.

CivetWeb could also silently accept a partially parsed header section.
parse_http_headers() stopped after MG_MAX_HEADERS fields without rejecting
additional fields, and some malformed field lines could be mistaken for the
end of the header section. Framing fields such as Content-Length or
Transfer-Encoding appearing after that point could therefore escape the
validation performed by get_request().

The local fix strictly parses Content-Length as decimal digits with optional
trailing HTTP whitespace, rejects values above INT64_MAX, and rejects
duplicate Content-Length fields. It rejects requests combining a non-identity
Transfer-Encoding with Content-Length, rejects duplicate Transfer-Encoding
fields, rejects duplicate Host fields for all HTTP versions, and requires
exactly one Host field for HTTP/1.1 requests. It also rejects malformed field
lines and header sections exceeding MG_MAX_HEADERS instead of silently
accepting a partially parsed request. Invalid requests return HTTP 400 before
routing and REST processing.

Local commit:
https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon/commit/1d6e4a74

Upstream commit:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/commit/588860e3

The upstream commit introduced the intended Transfer-Encoding/Content-Length
check but also introduced compile errors in get_request(). PR #1432 contains
the corrected form of that block. The additional strict Content-Length,
duplicate Transfer-Encoding, Host, and complete header-section validation are
local hardening.

Upstream PR #1432:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1432

Related issue #1408:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1408

Related PR #1400:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1400

CVE: none identified for these specific HTTP request validation fixes. They
must not be attributed to CVE-2025-55763, which is a different URI parser
problem.

2. Percent-encoded URI control characters
-----------------------------------------

Severity: SECURITY RELEVANT; no CVSS or CVE identified

CivetWeb previously accepted percent-encoded ASCII control characters in
request URIs. Upstream PR #1341 added explicit rejection of %0d and %0a to
prevent CRLF injection, and that fix is present in the upstream baseline
imported by snapraid-daemon.

Other encoded control characters were still accepted. In particular, %00 was
decoded to a NUL byte in place, truncating the URI as a C string before later
URI normalization, handler matching, and authorization processing. Encoded
control characters %01 through %1f and %7f could likewise reach later URI
processing despite their literal forms being rejected.

The local follow-up generalizes PR #1341. URI validation now rejects all
percent-encoded ASCII control characters from 0x00 through 0x1f and 0x7f. It
also rejects malformed percent-encodings instead of allowing them to reach
later URI processing.

Local commit:
https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon/commit/43ad1aeb

Upstream PR #1341:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1341

CVE: none identified for these fixes.


NOT APPLICABLE
==============

1. CVE-2025-55763: URI parser buffer overflow
---------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH, CVSS 3.1 7.5

The upstream report describes a remotely triggerable buffer overflow in
CivetWeb URI processing, with memory corruption and denial-of-service impact
and reports of possible code execution.

Why not applicable: the affected path belongs to CivetWeb built-in
filesystem/file-serving URI handling. snapraid-daemon is built with NO_FILES
and NO_FILESYSTEMS, and the affected file-serving path is absent from the
stripped source.

Upstream issue #1356:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1356

CVE:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-55763

2. CVE-2025-9648: mg_handle_form_request() infinite loop
------------------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH, CVSS 4.0 8.7

A crafted form POST containing a NUL byte can make mg_handle_form_request()
loop indefinitely, causing remote CPU denial of service.

Why not applicable: snapraid-daemon does not include CivetWeb form handling.
mg_handle_form_request() and handle_form.inl are absent from the stripped
source.

Upstream issue #1348:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1348

Upstream fix commit:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/commit/782e1890

CVE:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-9648

3. Multipart form stack-buffer overflow
--------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH, reported CVSS 3.1 7.5

Upstream issue #1404 reports a remotely triggerable stack-buffer overflow in
mg_handle_form_request() while parsing a crafted multipart request. Two
arithmetic errors can produce an extremely large memmove() against an 8 KiB
stack buffer.

Why not applicable: the form-handling implementation has been removed from the
local CivetWeb copy.

Upstream issue #1404:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1404

CVE: none identified for this report at the review baseline.

4. Additional form parser underflow and infinite loop
---------------------------------------------------

Severity: SIGNIFICANT, no assigned CVSS identified

PR #1411 reports two remotely triggerable bugs in mg_handle_form_request(): an
integer underflow that can produce an invalid huge value length and an endless
loop on a truncated URL-encoded body ending in an unconsumable fragment.

Why not applicable: the entire CivetWeb form parser is absent from the
stripped snapraid-daemon source.

Upstream PR #1411:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1411

CVE: none identified for these exact fixes at the review baseline.

5. CVE-2026-29035: WebSocket permessage-deflate overflow
------------------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH, CVSS 4.0 8.3

A crafted compressed WebSocket frame can make read_websocket() append the
four-byte zlib sync trailer past the end of a buffer. The published impact
includes heap corruption, denial of service, and possible code execution.

Why not applicable: the vulnerable path requires WebSocket support, zlib, and
experimental interfaces. The stripped build has USE_WEBSOCKET=0, USE_ZLIB=0,
and MG_EXPERIMENTAL_INTERFACES=0.

Upstream issue #1381:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1381

Related upstream issue #1379:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1379

CVE:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-29035

6. HTTP/2 memory-safety and state-reuse problems
-----------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH as reported; no CVE identified for this group

Upstream issue #1379 and PR #1409 describe multiple HTTP/2 problems, including
stale per-request state, invalid buffer offsets, freeing stale header
pointers, HPACK encoding failures, and related memory-safety conditions.

Why not applicable: snapraid-daemon explicitly disables USE_HTTP2. The HTTP/2
implementation is removed or unreachable in the stripped build.

Upstream issue #1379:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1379

Upstream PR #1409:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1409

7. HTTP/2 missing :path NULL dereference
---------------------------------------

Severity: SIGNIFICANT, remote unauthenticated denial of service

An HTTP/2 HEADERS frame without the mandatory :path pseudo-header can leave
request_uri NULL. The HTTP request path then dereferences it, crashing the
worker.

Why not applicable: USE_HTTP2 is disabled and the HTTP/2 path is not part of
the snapraid-daemon build.

Upstream issue #1428:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1428

Upstream PR #1431:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1431

CVE: none identified at the review baseline.

8. protect_uri case-sensitive authentication bypass
-----------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH in affected upstream deployments

Upstream protect_uri matching uses a case-sensitive comparison. On a
case-insensitive filesystem, a differently cased path can therefore miss the
protection rule while still resolving to the protected resource.

Why not applicable: snapraid-daemon does not use CivetWeb protect_uri
authentication. The stripped check_authorization() implementation is a stub,
filesystem serving is disabled, and REST authentication is provided through
mg_set_auth_handler().

Upstream issue #1429:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1429

Upstream PR #1430:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/pull/1430

CVE: none identified at the review baseline.

9. CVE-2026-5789: Windows unquoted service path
---------------------------------------------

Severity: HIGH, CVSS 4.0 8.5

CivetWeb 1.16 can install its Windows service using an unquoted executable
path. A local attacker able to place an executable in a searched path may gain
elevated code execution.

Why not applicable: this concerns the CivetWeb standalone Windows service
installer. snapraid-daemon embeds the stripped CivetWeb library and does not
use the standalone Windows service executable.

Upstream issue #1390:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1390

Related upstream commit:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/commit/3c0fb6ad

CVE:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-5789

10. CVE-2025-57052: cJSON array index validation
-----------------------------------------------

Severity: CRITICAL, CVSS 3.1 9.8

cJSON versions through 1.7.18 can perform an out-of-bounds access while
decoding malformed JSON pointer array indexes.

Why not applicable: CivetWeb carries cJSON only for examples, and
snapraid-daemon does not vendor or use that cJSON component. Its REST JSON
parser is unrelated.

Upstream issue #1426:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1426

CVE:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-57052

11. Lua CVE-2021-44964 and CVE-2022-28805
------------------------------------------

Severity: MEDIUM 6.3 and CRITICAL 9.1 respectively

The bundled Lua versions discussed upstream are affected by a
use-after-free/sandbox-escape issue and a parser heap over-read affecting
untrusted Lua code.

Why not applicable: snapraid-daemon has USE_LUA=0 and the stripped CivetWeb
build does not include the Lua execution subsystem.

Upstream issue #1425:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1425

CVE-2021-44964:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2021-44964

CVE-2022-28805:
https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2022-28805

Lua fix commit for CVE-2022-28805:
https://github.com/lua/lua/commit/1f3c6f45

12. Slow HTTP attack through chunked request bodies
---------------------------------------------------

Severity: DENIAL-OF-SERVICE concern upstream; no CVE identified

Upstream issue #1353 reports that mg_read() can spend excessive time consuming
a slowly delivered chunked request body, allowing a client to hold server
resources for an extended period.

Why not applicable: snapraid-daemon rejects chunked JSON requests before its
REST handlers call mg_read(). CivetWeb keep-alive is also left disabled, so
handled requests have must_close set and the framework does not call
discard_unread_request_data() to drain a slowly delivered body after the
handler returns.

Upstream issue #1353:
https://github.com/civetweb/civetweb/issues/1353

CVE: none identified for this report.

